<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gathas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gāthās</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gathas are the central texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, composed in Old or Gathic Avestan and often attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zarathustra</a> himself. They comprise five poems of seventeen hymns, with 238 stanzas and 897 verses, and survive within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasna">Yasna</a> liturgy. They are the oldest attestation of an Iranian language. Scholars are divided on whether Zarathustra wrote them. Zoroastrian tradition places him in the 6th century BCE, but many scholars now accept a date near 1000 BCE, some as early as 1500 BCE. The texts describe a simple society of nomadic pastoralists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gathas.jpg" alt="Gathas" width="330" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodleian_J2_fol_175_Y_28_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zarathustra</a> is connected to the Gathas already within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a>: he is the person most often named in them, and the Young Avestan texts describe him reciting them. Kellens and Skjærvø have argued strongly against his authorship, while Humbach and Hintze have reaffirmed it. The Gathas contain nothing that links them to known historical events. Their internal evidence shows a simple society based on nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a>, with horses and Bactrian camels prominent in personal names, and scholars connect them with the movement of Iranian tribes into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> in the mid to late 2nd millennium BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The five Gathas are the Ahunavaiti Gatha (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasna">Yasna</a> 28 to 34), the Ushtavaiti Gatha (Yasna 43 to 46), the Spentamainyu Gatha (Yasna 47 to 50), the Vohukhshathra Gatha (Yasna 51) and the Vahishtoishti Gatha (Yasna 53). Each has its own metre. Opinions on the exact content still diverge, and translations are often seen as interpretations, partly because the corpus contains many words found only once. Stausberg describes a theological and biographical framework, which reads the Vahishtoishti Gatha as a wedding sermon for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zarathustra</a>'s youngest daughter Pourucista, and a ritualistic one begun by Humbach, in which the audience is the god Mazda.</p><p>There is consensus on some themes. Mazda is the creator of everything good, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zarathustra</a> is the link between the faithful and Mazda. Humans must navigate the opposition between Asha (truth, harmony) and Druj (deception), and the Gathas oppose the daevas worshipped by the followers of Druj.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> contains the Gathas alone; they survive in liturgical manuscripts and are recited during the daily <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasna">Yasna</a> liturgy and the Visperad. Priests learned them by rote, and written transmission began only with the invention of the Avestan alphabet in the Sasanian period. The first modern critical edition was by Westergaard in 1852, superseded by Geldner in 1885, which remains the reference edition. The Gathas have been translated more often than any other Avestan text, beginning with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> translation that is considered poor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gathas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The language of the hymns attributed to him, the <b>Gathas</b>, is an archaic form of Avestan, an Iranian language closely related to the Sanskrit of the Rigveda, and the world they describe is one of herders and their cattle without cities or kings.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The nasks fell into three groups of seven: a Gathic group containing the <b>Gathas</b> and long commentaries on them, a manthric group, and a legal group.</blockquote><a href="/work/avesta">Avesta · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Its preserved archaic syntax and morphology matter for reconstructing Proto-Indo-European, and its hymns resemble the <b>Gathas</b> of old Avestan and Homer's Iliad.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
